{
  "teamId": 121,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 New York Mets entered the season with championship aspirations and a payroll to match, but the year became a slow-motion unraveling of expectations. After a promising April that saw the team finish 14-11, the roster showed flashes of its immense talent — young catcher Francisco Alvarez announced himself as a star with a series of clutch hits and home runs, and Pete Alonso continued his assault on opposing pitchers. But for every moment of brilliance, the bullpen found a way to give it back, with David Robertson and Adam Ottavino repeatedly surrendering leads in high-leverage situations.\n\nJune was the season's true breaking point. A catastrophic 7-19 month buried the Mets in the standings and effectively ended any realistic playoff hopes before the calendar flipped to July. The bullpen's implosion was almost systematic — Robertson allowed grand slams and walk-off home runs with startling regularity, while the offense went cold at the worst possible moments. By the time the team emerged from that June wreckage, the damage was irreparable.\n\nJuly brought a surprising resurgence, a 15-9 month that offered a painful glimpse of what this team could have been. Alvarez kept mashing, Mark Canha contributed timely extra-base hits, and the offense remembered it had 215 home runs in its arsenal. But the good times couldn't hold — August cratered again at 11-18, with the bullpen continuing to self-destruct against Texas and others down the stretch. Trevor Gott's late-season disasters symbolized a relief corps that never found stability all year.\n\nSeptember offered a bittersweet coda. The Mets played respectable ball at 14-13, with Pete Alonso launching his 45th home run and Brandon Nimmo providing clutch doubles, but it was all meaningless in the standings. A team that spent $340 million on Max Scherzer and Justin Verlander, then watched injuries and inconsistency consume them, finished 75-87 — a dozen games below .500 and far from the October stage they were built for. The 2023 Mets will be remembered as one of baseball's great disappointments, a cautionary tale about how quickly a contender can dissolve.",
